WEBVTT

00:00.050 --> 00:05.473
CAMP: Cooperative Arm–Hand Motion Planning in Constrained Spaces

00:05.617 --> 00:10.093
Constrained manipulation requires arm–hand coordination.

00:10.093 --> 00:18.790
Separate planning can miss feasible motions, while full joint planning faces high-dimensional search and nonconvex collision constraints.

00:18.790 --> 00:22.211
We call this the Arm–Hand Planning Dilemma.

00:22.350 --> 00:32.168
We formulate planning using feasible hand fibers: sets of hand configurations that keep the complete system collision-free for a given arm configuration.

00:32.168 --> 00:35.563
As the arm moves, these fibers change.

00:35.563 --> 00:39.050
A fixed hand shape may therefore become infeasible.

00:39.050 --> 00:44.063
Our goal is to find a continuous arm–hand path from start to goal.

00:44.533 --> 00:49.086
We now present six simulation tasks with different spatial constraints.

00:49.086 --> 00:53.097
Colored dotted curves show wrist and fingertip trajectories.

00:53.097 --> 00:56.915
Gray marks the start pose, and green marks the target pose.

00:57.467 --> 01:04.200
We begin with Wall Traversal and Multi-Sphere Avoidance, showing coordinated motion around different obstacles.

01:05.033 --> 01:11.962
Next, Narrow Passage Traversal requires the complete system to pass between upper and lower obstacles.

01:11.962 --> 01:16.913
The arm posture and hand shape change together as the robot moves toward the target.

01:18.367 --> 01:23.496
Ball-in-Box Pregrasp coordinates the arm approach with finger motion through the opening.

01:23.996 --> 01:30.842
The fingers enter the box and reach the prescribed pregrasp pose, with no grasp performed in simulation.

01:32.633 --> 01:38.437
Display Button Press brings the index finger to the button while the arm avoids the display structure.

01:38.437 --> 01:45.103
Here, success means reaching the target fingertip position, without physically pressing the button.

01:46.900 --> 01:50.515
The final simulation task is Cabinet Bottle Pregrasp.

01:50.765 --> 01:59.593
The hand becomes compact to enter the cabinet opening, passes the barrier, and reopens around the bottle at the prescribed pregrasp configuration.

02:01.300 --> 02:07.254
These four examples show obstacle collisions, self-collision, and premature target collision.

02:07.254 --> 02:10.161
Each view freezes when a collision occurs.

02:10.161 --> 02:15.082
Red circles mark the affected region, while the labels identify the cause.

02:16.250 --> 02:18.542
Real-robot experiments follow.

02:18.716 --> 02:22.626
The first real-robot experiment is Ball-in-Box Grasp.

02:22.626 --> 02:27.652
The arm approaches the box and guides the hand through the square opening toward the ball.

02:27.816 --> 02:34.042
After reaching the target configuration, the hand closes around the ball and lifts it successfully.

02:34.983 --> 02:40.946
In Display Button Press, the arm moves around the barrier as the index finger approaches the button.

02:41.116 --> 02:46.829
No press is performed; success is fingertip arrival at the target button position.

02:48.850 --> 02:55.813
In Cabinet Bottle Grasp, the hand passes through the circular opening and reaches the target configuration.

02:56.383 --> 02:59.122
The hand then grasps and lifts the bottle.
